      "worked_solution": "Recall the definition of sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡U=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{ST}{SU}=\\frac{ST}{12.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $ST.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =\\sqrt{S{U}^{2}-T{U}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12.5)^{2}-(7.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{156.25-56.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡U & =\\frac{ST}{12.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{12.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
